The default unittest runner now runs each `unittest` block on its own

Previously all the `unittest` blocks of a module were run as one, so the
first failure skipped the remaining blocks of that module, and results were
counted in modules. The default runner now uses $(D ModuleInfo.unitTests) to
run every block individually and carries on after a failure.

Progress is reported on `stderr`, with one line per module followed by a
summary. A failing unittest is named before its failure is printed, with a
stack trace unless the failure is an assertion in the unittested module's own
file (see below):

```
pkg.mod.__unittest_L7_C1: FAILED
core.exception.AssertError@pkg/mod.d(9): boom
----------------
??:? [0x5608992fb290]
??:? [0x5608992fa64f]
...
pkg.mod: 1 passed, 1 FAILED
pkg.other: 1 passed
1/3 unittests FAILED
```

When an assertion fails in the file of a top-level module, the short
`file(line): [unittest] message` form is printed without a stack trace, as
before, but it is now written to `stderr` instead of `stdout`.

The summary wording changes from `N modules passed unittests` and
`F/N modules FAILED unittests` to `N unittests passed` and
`F/N unittests FAILED`, which matters to scripts matching the old text.

Because the remaining unittests still run after a failure, a unittest may now
run after an earlier one in the same module failed, where previously it was
skipped.

`main` is still only run when there are no unittests.

To revert to the previous runner, with unittests run and counted per module and
its output unchanged, pass `--DRT-testmode=test-or-main` to the program:

```
./app --DRT-testmode=test-or-main
```

or embed the option in the executable so that it applies to every run:

---
extern(C) __gshared string[] rt_options = ["testmode=test-or-main"];
---

`test-or-main` is what the runner did by default before this change. The other
`--DRT-testmode` values, `run-main` and `test-only`, also select the previous
runner and keep their meaning.

Custom handlers set with $(D Runtime.moduleUnitTester) or
$(D Runtime.extendedModuleUnitTester) are not affected.
